WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:08.330
- Hi everybody, I'm Maurice Samuels,

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:10.010
the director of the Yale Program

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:12.170
for the Study of Antisemitism

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:15.320
and it's my great pleasure
to welcome you here today

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.150
to the next in our Benjamin

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:20.020
and Barbara Zucker Lecture Series.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:23.580
It's an especially great pleasure
to welcome today's speaker

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.070
Adam Stern back to Yale.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:29.730
Some of you remember that Adam
was a post-doctoral fellow

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:31.680
in our program a few years ago

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.320
when he was finishing up the book

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.987
that he's gonna be
talking about today called

00:00:35.987 --> 00:00:39.680
"Survival: A
Theological-Political Genealogy"

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.410
which has now been released

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:43.900
by University of Pennsylvania press,

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:47.380
and I just received my copy yesterday.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.010
Adam received his BA from Columbia,

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:52.330
his MA from University of Chicago

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:55.580
and his PhD from Harvard
in the study of religion.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:58.120
He's now an assistant professor of German

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.780
at the University of Wisconsin, Madison,

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.650
where he teaches courses
on continental philosophy,

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.830
Jewish thought, race and
religion, secularism,

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:10.770
political theology, translation
theory and media studies.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.610
He's gonna be in conversation
today with my colleague

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:16.410
in the German department, Paul North.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.780
Paul is well known to most of you.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.100
Among other things, he's the author

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:24.610
of "The Yield: Kafka's
Atheological Reformation"

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:27.000
published by Stanford in 2015,

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.290
and a new book that I have
to say sounds incredibly cool

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:33.920
called "Bizarre-Privileged
Items in the Universe:

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.670
The Logic of Likeness",

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:39.500
literally just out last
week from zone books.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.500
So here's how things are gonna work,

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.930
Adam is gonna present his
book for about 10 minutes,

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:47.540
then Paul will engage him in conversation,

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:50.580
then we'll open it up to
questions from the audience.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.440
Please submit questions
using the Q&amp;A function

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.750
at the bottom of your screen, not chat.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:01.140
You can submit questions at any
time during the presentation

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.410
and Paul will ask them,

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:06.290
and we're gonna finish promptly at six.

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:11.290
So I will now mute my video
and hand it over to Adam.

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:14.200
- Thanks very much Maurice
for the kind introduction

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.430
and for hosting this event and,

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:20.870
also to Paul North for
agreeing to participate

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:25.400
and to Inessa Laskova for
helping to organize the event.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:26.455
So as Maurice said,

00:02:26.455 --> 00:02:28.550
I'm just gonna speak for a few minutes

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:32.750
and then we'll proceed
with some discussion.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:35.770
So my book began with a few observations.

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:39.400
The first was that survival
occupies a prominent place

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.323
within the modern, secular
political imagination.

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:44.570
When you only think of the ways

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:45.980
that the figure of the survivor

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:49.600
has come to mediate the
representation of current events

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.690
from genocide, climate
catastrophe and mass shootings

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:56.070
and disaster capitalism to
nuclear war, terminal illness

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:59.163
and perhaps in the post-Holocaust
present everyday life.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.700
The second was that
one could also identify

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.290
a popular association
between survival and Jews.

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:08.640
Considered here not only the primacy,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.460
accorded to the Holocaust
survivor in our time

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.180
but also the extent to which survival

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:16.130
underwrites the narration
of both Jewish history,

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:18.540
the survival of the
Jews and Jewish politics

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:20.433
the survival of the Israeli state.

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:23.670
The question I wanted to ask was this,

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.730
what if anything unites
these two horizons,

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:29.400
could an examination of secular survival

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.810
shed light on the issue
of Jewish survival?

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:33.830
And what could a study of Jewish survival

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:36.703
tell us about the dissemination
of survival more broadly?

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.830
It became clear at an
early stage in the research

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:43.040
that any attempt to write this history

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:46.750
would have to go beyond
two major themes or areas,

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:48.970
Darwinism and the Holocaust.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:51.950
Neither biology nor biopolitics
seem to fully account

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:55.040
for the theological and
theological political resonances

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.500
of the discourse on Jewish survival.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:58.690
My hunch moreover,

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:02.150
was that any
theological-political perspective

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:04.350
on survival or Jewish survival

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:07.000
would have to acknowledge
the code segmented

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.870
by the asymmetric legacy of
the Jewish-Christian debate.

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:13.230
So this gave rise to a hypothesis,

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:15.400
perhaps the link between Jewish survival

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:19.010
and secular survival could be
found in the invisible space

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:21.540
between and in the very
thing that otherwise

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:24.090
managed to erase itself from view,

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:26.663
the theological-political
history of Christianity.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:29.890
So this book interrogates the possibility

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:35.250
that Latin Christianity has
played an overwhelming role

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:38.160
in shaping survival as a
word, theme and figure.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:38.993
More than that,

00:04:38.993 --> 00:04:40.990
the book is an attempt to
follow the language of survival

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:43.600
as it modulates,
disarticulates, and rearranges

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:45.320
a number of categorical distinctions

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:47.490
between things like the
secular, the Jewish,

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:49.390
the Judeo-Christian and the Christian.

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:54.430
My strategy throughout is to read secular

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:56.660
and/or Jewish writers who have contributed

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:59.360
or been made to contribute

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:02.970
to the vast proliferation
of survival talk.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:05.630
What I tend to show is
that these arguments

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:09.500
that appear to highlight the
secular and/or Jewish signature

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:13.160
of survival also consent
and even demand attention

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:15.520
to a different set of readings.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:17.730
Again and again, they return survival

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:19.880
to a Latinate field of translation,

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:23.210
to scenes of incarnation,
passion and resurrection.

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:25.190
In short, to a stage dominated

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:28.313
by the sovereign triumphalism
of Christ's body.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:31.780
In this sense, the book is less a history

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:34.720
of survival than a genealogy of its force.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:35.940
And I'll talk a little bit more

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:39.060
about what I mean by
genealogy in a minute.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:40.180
It doesn't seek to address

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:43.540
the whole of survival's manifold uses,

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:45.890
forge a linear narrative
of its development

00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:47.250
or contextualize it

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:50.040
in reference to a single historical event.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:53.360
Instead, the book moves
backward from the present

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:56.420
in order to rewrite the
interpretive regimes

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:59.470
that have dominated survival circulation.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:02.860
These genealogies of
survival take major texts

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:04.980
of 20th century philosophy, theology

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:09.730
and political theory as sites
or occasions for collecting

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:11.940
and constellating the scattered elements

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.723
of survival's
theological-political archive.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:19.290
My hope in the book is that
this will establish a framework

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:21.340
for thinking survival
as a Christian question

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:24.130
and in doing so offer
a critical assessment

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:26.700
of Christianity's role in
the constitution of modern,

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:28.763
secular and Jewish politics.

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:33.250
In order to frame all of these issues,

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:36.150
I begin with a single question, which is,

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:38.303
who is speaking about survival?

00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:40.930
And the question seeks to highlight

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:43.293
the genealogical objective of the study.

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:48.720
My central purpose is not
to affirm or deny survival,

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:50.960
it's not to accept or reject its rhetoric

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:54.400
or to say yes or no to
the stances it defines.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:58.900
Instead following Michel
Foucault's definition of genealogy,

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:01.010
or at least one of them the task

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:05.080
is to gauge the fact that
survival is spoken about,

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:07.410
to discover who does the speaking,

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:10.390
the position and viewpoints
from which they speak,

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:13.970
the institutions which prompt
people to speak about survival

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:17.410
and which store and distribute
the things that are said.

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:18.243
In other words,

00:07:18.243 --> 00:07:21.510
I use genealogy as a
scheme for provincializing

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:24.850
the presumed universality of these codes

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:29.830
and ask how speech that has
not always been self-evident

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:33.023
to everyone has been made to
look obvious and universal.

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:38.200
My goal that is, is to say
that genealogy of survival

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:41.590
is not a history of survival,
but instead a way of asking

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:43.840
where such a history might begin?

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:44.910
What are survival's limits?

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:45.880
What are its archives?

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:47.220
What are its languages?

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:50.690
And importantly, in the book
what field of translations

00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:52.893
mediate its generalization?

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:57.500
So the genealogy presented
over the course of the book

00:07:57.500 --> 00:07:59.500
is at best a prologue to a history

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:01.873
that has not really been written.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:05.310
Though restrictive and partial,

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:07.460
the aspirational constraints acknowledge

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:10.980
the need for a preparatory
inventory of the major questions,

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:15.100
texts and structures of
speaking that have made survival

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:18.963
an identifiable element of
our current political horizon.

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:26.190
In doing this, my preliminary hypothesis

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:30.480
is that the genealogy
of survival should begin

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:32.360
with the specific political

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:34.210
and theological-political anxieties

00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:37.410
surrounding the survival of the Jews.

00:08:37.410 --> 00:08:39.510
In choosing this point of departure,

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:42.740
I'm not arguing for a direct
and transparent return

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:45.120
to the Jewish textual tradition,

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.720
but rather for a more complex encounter

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:51.470
with the vicissitudes of
history and translation

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:53.130
that have contributed to the adoption

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:55.850
of this language by both Jews and others.

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:57.970
The political theorist Robert Meister

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:00.740
motions in a helpful
direction here, I think,

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:03.730
by speculating that the
political prominence

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:05.970
of Jewish survival is as much about Jews

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:07.890
as it is about the Christian West

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:09.890
and the history of the Jewish question

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:14.223
that goes as far back as Paul,
why are there still Jews?

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:19.500
Now this insight could take
the genealogy of survival

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:21.023
in a number of directions.

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:25.930
On the one hand, a shared
concern with Jewish survival

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:27.930
could serve as a means of reconciling

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:31.550
the commonalities of (indistinct)

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:36.080
that runs from the Bible
to the present day.

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:38.540
On the other hand, survival
could also be an opportunity

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:42.090
to explore the historical grammar concepts

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:44.150
underlying secularism.

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:47.513
Critics of secularism like
Talal Asad for instance,

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:50.630
have argued that the
Judeo-Christian heritage

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:53.410
assumed by modern secularity

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:56.260
is actually a retrospective reconstruction

00:09:56.260 --> 00:10:00.270
whose vision of unity has
particular political stakes,

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:04.367
in Asad's word, "the grammatical
exclusion of Muslims".

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:07.790
The problem is that
neither Meister nor Asad

00:10:09.043 --> 00:10:10.930
ever really divest
themselves of the suspicion

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:14.240
that the secular embrace
of a Judeo-Christian past

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:15.890
is really just the reiteration

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:19.230
of an older Christian perspective
on the theological place

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:20.780
of the Jews and Christiandom

00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:22.860
that it's a kind of re-description,

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:25.803
a super sessional re-description
of Christianity itself.

00:10:28.710 --> 00:10:31.630
In this light, I approached your survival

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:34.860
neither as a Jewish nor as
a Judeo-Christian question

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:36.860
but instead as a counterintuitive index

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:38.410
for the way that survival

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:44.560
explains, refracts the
secularized forms of Christianity

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:47.250
that marked modernity in the West.

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:51.000
Of course, versions of
disclaim are by now familiar.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:54.960
Few philosophers of religion,
for example would be surprised

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:57.080
to learn that Christianity provides

00:10:57.080 --> 00:10:59.673
the inescapable backdrop
to Western philosophy.

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:02.290
The same statement could apply

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:05.220
as much to individual
thinkers explicitly involved

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:06.770
in projects like detheolization

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:11.240
as it could to more abstract
and far reaching endeavors

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:14.210
to unearth the theological traces at play

00:11:14.210 --> 00:11:16.603
in something like modern
political economy.

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:21.250
And yet the stark obviousness
of Christianity significance

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:24.190
has not necessarily led
to any further clarity

00:11:24.190 --> 00:11:27.923
on the kind of object or
subject that Christianity is.

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:31.700
Which is to say that even
the most sophisticated

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:33.840
genealogical work sometimes struggles

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:36.700
to contain the slippages
between Christianity

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:39.243
and a host of other
historically freighted terms,

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:43.590
not the least of which
would be the word religion.

00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:46.363
This translation between
Christianity and religion.

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:51.610
In order to open this
up in a different way,

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:55.560
I borrow from a term
that Dr. Derrida coined

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:59.510
toward the end of his career
namely global Latinization,

00:11:59.510 --> 00:12:01.490
is kind of neo-liberalism

00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:05.300
that attempts to combine
Latin Christianity

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:09.570
with the century's long
process of universalization

00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:12.483
that Derrida also calls globalization.

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:16.270
Which it also implies,

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:19.720
for me with this term
global Latinization implies,

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:22.440
is that the question I've been asking

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:25.900
who is speaking about religion
cannot ignore the fact

00:12:25.900 --> 00:12:28.853
that the name survival as
with the name religion,

00:12:29.781 --> 00:12:32.220
we are already speaking Latin.

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:35.140
So my genealogy begins
also as an investigation

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:37.640
into the history of translation

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:41.750
as it makes its way from Rome
in terms like supravietuire,

00:12:41.750 --> 00:12:45.600
to Europe, survival, (indistinct)

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:48.940
to the United States in
English again survival

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:53.083
and has become part again of
our current political horizon.

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:56.830
To borrow a quote from Saba Mahmood,

00:12:56.830 --> 00:12:59.170
one might say that survival is symptomatic

00:12:59.170 --> 00:13:02.860
of the fundamental centrality
of Christian norms,

00:13:02.860 --> 00:13:04.700
values and sensibilities,

00:13:04.700 --> 00:13:07.130
However Judaic they are made out to be

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:10.113
to European conceptions of
what it means to be secular.

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:14.320
So, one final question, before I end,

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:16.860
how does this work in the book?

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:21.270
As I've said, I look at a
series of canonical writers,

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:23.070
Hannah Arendt, Walter Benjamin,

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:25.543
Franz Rosenzweig and Sigmund Freud.

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:28.140
And again the question is why

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:31.490
these canonical and familiar names?

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:33.653
So just a few comments on that choice.

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:38.260
In choosing them, I'm
compelled by two strategies.

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:40.920
First, because of the
work of Arendt, Rosenzweig

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:43.710
and Freud has been so
central to the post-war

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:46.020
expression of Jewish survival,

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:48.220
their writings are also effective sites

00:13:48.220 --> 00:13:51.560
for provisionally
disarticulating these two terms,

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:52.763
Jewish and survival.

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:55.740
Second, because of the writings of Arendt,

00:13:55.740 --> 00:13:56.660
Benjamin and Freud

00:13:56.660 --> 00:14:00.720
have circulated well beyond the
discourse on Jewish survival

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:02.300
and become major resources

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:04.460
in the propagation of survival talk

00:14:04.460 --> 00:14:08.970
across geographic and topical contexts,

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:12.060
they can also serve as
occasions for reorienting

00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:17.580
along global Latin lines,
survival's theological,

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:19.283
political genealogy.

00:14:21.490 --> 00:14:24.300
Just two quick examples.

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:27.780
In the chapter, actually
we'll stay with one example,

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:29.680
in the chapter on Arendt which begins,

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:34.880
the claim is survival.

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:38.357
This word survival runs like
a red thread through the book,

00:14:38.357 --> 00:14:40.140
"The Origins of Totalitarianism"

00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:42.130
stringing together it's
intersecting analyses

00:14:42.130 --> 00:14:45.340
of Antisemitism imperialism
and totalitarianism.

00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:47.360
And so through a comparative
reading of these sections

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:50.868
and the respective representations
of Jewish survival,

00:14:50.868 --> 00:14:51.960
what Arendt calls Jewish survival,

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:55.130
African survival and European survival,

00:14:55.130 --> 00:14:58.530
I establish Arendt's text as
a kind of digest for thinking

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:02.103
about survival's
theological-political archive.

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:07.650
I do this across each section
until the story culminates

00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:09.660
in a striking image that Arendt borrows

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:13.010
from a number of figures in her own time

00:15:13.010 --> 00:15:15.560
that finds the description
of the concentration

00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:20.560
camps in which the figure
of the survivor is Lazarus,

00:15:21.730 --> 00:15:25.050
saved and resurrected from
the ruin of a ghostly realm

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:28.290
that Arendt herself and a
kind of racialized narrative

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:31.510
that has been picked up by
others continually compares

00:15:31.510 --> 00:15:33.710
to "the dark continent".

00:15:33.710 --> 00:15:36.570
And so survival becomes a way
of thinking about how a series

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:40.880
of things, religion, race,
sovereignty and so forth

00:15:41.720 --> 00:15:43.670
can be thought within the history

00:15:43.670 --> 00:15:46.410
of a Christian
theological-political archive

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:48.963
and what some of those
elements might look like.

00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:51.140
I will stop here

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:55.043
and we can begin the discussion.

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:00.157
- Thanks Adam.

00:16:00.157 --> 00:16:01.680
- [Adam] Yeah.

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:03.947
- Well, there's a lot to
take up in what you say

00:16:03.947 --> 00:16:06.530
and a lot to take up in the book.

00:16:06.530 --> 00:16:08.020
I appreciate having read it

00:16:08.020 --> 00:16:13.020
and I appreciate the attempt
to save Jews from survival.

00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:17.140
It's a loaded term as you show,

00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:19.500
it's loaded in a whole
bunch of different ways.

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:23.430
In fact, it seems as you read
through the book you discover

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:25.550
that it is connected to so many different

00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:31.400
problematic projects, you could
say that I begin to wonder

00:16:31.770 --> 00:16:33.793
and this is my first question to you.

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:36.270
It's a general question

00:16:36.270 --> 00:16:38.620
and then I wanna get to
some specifics in the book.

00:16:38.620 --> 00:16:39.913
What's the alternative?

00:16:40.830 --> 00:16:45.390
Have you thought of the real
work that this term does

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:49.500
for people in valorizing their experience?

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:51.070
I see it as,

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:53.490
and I'm with you in your
critiques all the way through,

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:55.513
I can see exactly where it comes from.

00:16:57.200 --> 00:17:01.030
But I see it as an indicator
of an unsettled relationship

00:17:01.030 --> 00:17:04.900
to finitude that as the
accounts have not been settled

00:17:04.900 --> 00:17:09.530
on one's own limitations and
also on the death of others

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:14.180
and survival is as you show
at times a triumphant response

00:17:14.180 --> 00:17:16.750
and at times a melancholic response

00:17:16.750 --> 00:17:19.000
and it oscillates between the two,

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:20.873
but it does do that work.

00:17:26.070 --> 00:17:26.903
- Right.

00:17:26.903 --> 00:17:31.100
So yes, this is a crucial
question, I think,

00:17:31.100 --> 00:17:33.940
which I'm understanding in two ways.

00:17:33.940 --> 00:17:37.630
One is that the way that survival captures

00:17:37.630 --> 00:17:40.313
both these triumphant moments
and this melancholic moments

00:17:40.313 --> 00:17:43.840
or you can say ghostly moments as I talk

00:17:43.840 --> 00:17:46.400
about in some portions of the book.

00:17:46.400 --> 00:17:50.610
And so if survival doing
this kind of important work,

00:17:50.610 --> 00:17:53.920
if it's capturing not
just one particular figure

00:17:53.920 --> 00:17:55.900
but a whole range of possibilities

00:17:55.900 --> 00:17:57.420
say it in a reaction to finitude

00:17:57.420 --> 00:17:59.490
or reaction to the death of others,

00:17:59.490 --> 00:18:01.410
then is there an alternative

00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:05.410
or should we hold onto
survival for some reason?

00:18:05.410 --> 00:18:07.280
And I think that's completely possible.

00:18:07.280 --> 00:18:08.650
It's possible to make an argument

00:18:08.650 --> 00:18:10.170
that we should hold on to survival

00:18:10.170 --> 00:18:11.960
as doing some kind of work.

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:15.000
My sense is that actually

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:18.380
even in places like the popular media,

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:21.840
there's something pushing against survival

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:24.170
and even the figure of the survivor

00:18:24.170 --> 00:18:28.180
not simply because of the content

00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:29.870
that it holds

00:18:29.870 --> 00:18:34.230
but also perhaps because of
the assets that it creates

00:18:34.230 --> 00:18:37.990
or the obviousness of the
terms that it brings together.

00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:41.110
For one thing, I think it still is related

00:18:41.110 --> 00:18:43.620
to a kind of post-Holocaust discourse that

00:18:47.650 --> 00:18:50.550
has certain familiar
images, certain familiar

00:18:52.913 --> 00:18:56.960
figurations that have come
to color all sorts of things

00:18:56.960 --> 00:18:58.270
as I point out in the introduction

00:18:58.270 --> 00:19:00.543
related to the cold war and so forth.

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:06.122
And the question is whether
beginning elsewhere is possible.

00:19:06.122 --> 00:19:09.980
And this is something that I
suggest at the end of the book

00:19:09.980 --> 00:19:13.970
with an attempt, with a kind
of displacement of translation

00:19:13.970 --> 00:19:16.173
to think outside the history of,

00:19:20.230 --> 00:19:23.020
Latin Christianity and
in relation to a series,

00:19:23.020 --> 00:19:26.080
kind of figure of translation
in Hebrew and Arabic

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:30.170
that would provide not
necessarily a postwar American

00:19:30.170 --> 00:19:34.397
or post-Holocaust orientation

00:19:34.397 --> 00:19:37.630
but maybe a kind of post-colonial
decolonial orientation

00:19:37.630 --> 00:19:41.200
to thinking about some of the
similar questions of finitude

00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:43.060
and so forth that you bring up.

00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:45.690
Just one other example though,
I think survival also creates

00:19:45.690 --> 00:19:47.370
a kind of problematic bind.

00:19:47.370 --> 00:19:48.290
There are two recent books

00:19:48.290 --> 00:19:50.100
that I've found helpful
in thinking about this.

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:52.790
One is a kind of ongoing
project summarized recently

00:19:52.790 --> 00:19:55.910
by Muhammad Mamdani
where he tries to suggest

00:19:55.910 --> 00:19:58.210
that what's so important about
the figure of the survivor

00:19:58.210 --> 00:20:02.810
in say post-genocidal context
is the way that it overcomes

00:20:02.810 --> 00:20:04.550
the difference between
victim and perpetrator

00:20:04.550 --> 00:20:05.790
and that it can actually, for that reason

00:20:05.790 --> 00:20:08.810
be a kind of reconciliatory
term for thinking about

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:10.583
those who have

00:20:12.230 --> 00:20:13.063
survived,

00:20:14.903 --> 00:20:17.240
a genocidal context.

00:20:17.240 --> 00:20:18.480
On the other hand, there's another book

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:21.610
that's come out recently by

00:20:22.814 --> 00:20:26.680
(indistinct) short tennis
scholar in Boston who suggests

00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:30.210
that the triumphalism of the
survivors always connected

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:32.120
somehow to settler colonial movements

00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:33.950
or the triumph of the survivor

00:20:33.950 --> 00:20:37.390
as the triumph of the
perpetrator, always in some way,

00:20:37.390 --> 00:20:40.210
this overcoming of victimhood,
the death of others,

00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:43.100
the denial of the pacivity of victimhood.

00:20:43.100 --> 00:20:45.010
And so for that reason,
you're caught somehow

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:48.030
in a double bind, what kind
of work is survival doing?

00:20:48.030 --> 00:20:49.890
Is it a kind of productive agency

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:54.720
or is it a denial of
another mode of subjectivity

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:57.813
that wouldn't have that
triumphalism kind of baked into it?

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:01.080
- Yeah, that's a great point.

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:04.450
Maybe we could talk
then about your reading

00:21:06.483 --> 00:21:07.733
of Agamben and,

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:11.097
the idea of the Muselmann.

00:21:14.222 --> 00:21:16.422
So the book takes you
through a whole series

00:21:17.438 --> 00:21:19.000
of versions of survival.

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:20.800
I can just read some of them,

00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:24.490
survival of, as you said, the "savage"

00:21:24.490 --> 00:21:27.470
which is a shocking
revelation about Arendt's

00:21:28.630 --> 00:21:32.000
thought of what she
calls the dark continent.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:36.280
The fortleben or the living
forth of texts in Benjamin,

00:21:36.280 --> 00:21:38.800
the imitation of Christ in Rosenzweig,

00:21:38.800 --> 00:21:42.550
the Eucharistic presence
of the absent body of God,

00:21:42.550 --> 00:21:46.530
which also shows up as a
gesture of media and so on,

00:21:46.530 --> 00:21:47.660
there's a number of them.

00:21:47.660 --> 00:21:52.573
But then you've got towards
the end to the question of,

00:21:54.060 --> 00:21:57.090
a social relationship where the survivor

00:21:57.090 --> 00:21:59.230
is always a survivor with relationship

00:21:59.230 --> 00:22:04.230
to someone who's life doesn't
count, even if they survive.

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:06.910
Would you talk a little bit about that?

00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:11.657
- Sure, thank Paul.

00:22:11.657 --> 00:22:15.050
Can you just tell me what
point you're referring to

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:17.980
at the end?

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:22.083
- I'm trying to find it
here in my various notes.

00:22:26.690 --> 00:22:28.690
I think I'm talking about...

00:22:32.540 --> 00:22:34.790
- I can answer in relationship
to Agamben if that helps.

00:22:34.790 --> 00:22:37.560
- Why don't you do that and
then I'll look for my...

00:22:37.560 --> 00:22:39.060
- I mean, so right.

00:22:39.060 --> 00:22:43.250
So, famously Agamben makes this argument

00:22:43.250 --> 00:22:47.330
about survival in
remnants of Auschwitz as a

00:22:47.330 --> 00:22:50.160
kind of actually intervention

00:22:50.160 --> 00:22:52.260
into this famous difference
that Foucault suggests

00:22:52.260 --> 00:22:53.900
between making die and making live,

00:22:53.900 --> 00:22:55.610
Agamben suggests to make survive

00:22:55.610 --> 00:22:58.130
as a kind of mechanism of differentiation

00:22:58.130 --> 00:23:00.150
between say survivors on the one hand

00:23:00.150 --> 00:23:04.560
and what he calls the Muselmann
or the Muslim in Auschwitz

00:23:04.560 --> 00:23:08.250
which has been of course
written about extensively.

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:12.370
And what I try to do
is not so much disagree

00:23:12.370 --> 00:23:17.370
with Agamben but suggest that
his own genealogical work

00:23:17.770 --> 00:23:19.340
in remnants of Auschwitz

00:23:19.340 --> 00:23:21.290
ends up preening kind of distinction

00:23:21.290 --> 00:23:24.373
between the theological, say,

00:23:25.521 --> 00:23:27.230
the remnant of Israel

00:23:27.230 --> 00:23:30.560
and the biopolitical which
he associates with survival.

00:23:30.560 --> 00:23:32.840
But that his actual genealogy of survival

00:23:32.840 --> 00:23:36.450
although alluding at moments
to a theological heritage

00:23:36.450 --> 00:23:40.060
never really doubles down on that

00:23:40.060 --> 00:23:41.600
and keeps these two things separate,

00:23:41.600 --> 00:23:43.853
the biopolitical and the theological.

00:23:44.690 --> 00:23:46.930
And what I try to suggest then is that,

00:23:46.930 --> 00:23:49.640
oddly enough, maybe not,

00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:53.350
oddly, I don't know, one can
actually think about the way

00:23:53.350 --> 00:23:57.540
that Franz Rosenzweig's
book written well before

00:23:57.540 --> 00:24:01.380
the context that Agamben is writing about

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:03.280
already seems to be

00:24:05.260 --> 00:24:08.460
working across similar lines in the sense

00:24:08.460 --> 00:24:11.690
that the survivor has this
triumphalist aspect to it

00:24:13.527 --> 00:24:15.310
that the Muslim is coded according

00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:17.820
to a particular history of despotism

00:24:17.820 --> 00:24:20.860
that marks the Muslim with death

00:24:20.860 --> 00:24:24.460
and that the entire discourse

00:24:24.460 --> 00:24:28.010
of survival in Rosenzweig's
book somehow circles

00:24:28.010 --> 00:24:32.330
around his understanding
of the body of Christ.

00:24:32.330 --> 00:24:34.533
And so in that sense,

00:24:35.470 --> 00:24:38.250
Rosenzweig's book becomes
a kind of supplement,

00:24:38.250 --> 00:24:42.170
I don't know, retrospective
supplement to Agamben's work

00:24:42.170 --> 00:24:44.070
to suggest that this isn't maybe about

00:24:46.890 --> 00:24:50.570
the Holocaust itself but
there's an older lineage

00:24:50.570 --> 00:24:52.950
for thinking about the way
that the language of survival

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:55.590
mediates our reception of things like

00:24:59.590 --> 00:25:02.630
those whose lives matter,
those whose lives don't matter

00:25:02.630 --> 00:25:04.313
as you put it earlier.

00:25:05.510 --> 00:25:06.970
- I see.

00:25:06.970 --> 00:25:10.040
So just to go a little
further with the distinction

00:25:10.040 --> 00:25:13.780
between the theo-political use of the term

00:25:13.780 --> 00:25:17.860
and reception from a
Christian theological past

00:25:17.860 --> 00:25:20.710
which you trace back to
Pseudo-Dionysius and Neoplatonism

00:25:23.095 --> 00:25:24.390
and the biopolitical,

00:25:24.390 --> 00:25:27.850
I can see very much the
stakes with the biopolitical

00:25:27.850 --> 00:25:31.990
which is a term invented by
Foucault in late lectures

00:25:31.990 --> 00:25:36.990
to talk about the way governments
manage biological life

00:25:39.370 --> 00:25:40.970
of the governed.

00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:45.680
And so the idea of survival
in a biopolitical realm

00:25:45.680 --> 00:25:48.840
could issue in violence
against the physical life

00:25:48.840 --> 00:25:50.653
of certain members of the community,

00:25:51.550 --> 00:25:53.960
the kind of logic of sacrifice there.

00:25:53.960 --> 00:25:56.223
What are the stakes of the theo-political?

00:25:57.230 --> 00:26:00.670
Is it just that people
are Crypto-Christians

00:26:00.670 --> 00:26:03.910
that kind Arendt or Benjamin, maybe less

00:26:03.910 --> 00:26:08.910
and they're waiting for an
afterlife, a perfect life?

00:26:09.480 --> 00:26:11.660
In some sense you suggest
this in Rosenzweig's,

00:26:11.660 --> 00:26:14.810
the end of Rosenzweig's book
where one goes into life

00:26:14.810 --> 00:26:18.740
that has to be sure and have
all its other characteristics.

00:26:18.740 --> 00:26:20.290
It's hard to know what that is.

00:26:21.460 --> 00:26:24.789
What are the stakes of the
theo-political reading?

00:26:24.789 --> 00:26:26.673
What are we losing there?

00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:33.330
- Well, let me answer
this question in two ways.

00:26:33.330 --> 00:26:36.430
So, the first thing to say
is that I don't know that

00:26:37.659 --> 00:26:38.820
Arendt, Benjamin,

00:26:38.820 --> 00:26:41.663
these figures are
exactly Crypto-Christian.

00:26:43.508 --> 00:26:45.100
At least my attempt is not to sort of make

00:26:45.100 --> 00:26:48.220
a challenge toward any
kind of identity claim.

00:26:48.220 --> 00:26:49.690
What I'm actually trying to suggest

00:26:49.690 --> 00:26:52.130
is that the identity claims
sometimes predetermines

00:26:52.130 --> 00:26:54.670
or overdetermines the way
that we read these texts.

00:26:54.670 --> 00:26:57.810
And that in fact, one
can read books by Arendt,

00:26:57.810 --> 00:27:01.630
by Benjamin and so forth
as helping to assemble

00:27:01.630 --> 00:27:03.840
this theological-political archive,

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:07.823
especially in or particularly
in regard to survival.

00:27:08.810 --> 00:27:10.870
The stakes, I think,

00:27:10.870 --> 00:27:13.200
if I'm understanding
your question correctly,

00:27:13.200 --> 00:27:17.370
and as I was suggesting as
well in the opening remarks

00:27:17.370 --> 00:27:21.800
is that survival was already
one could say theological,

00:27:21.800 --> 00:27:26.160
if you read the link
between Jews and survival

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:30.490
as marking survival in some
way with religion or theology.

00:27:30.490 --> 00:27:32.610
And in fact, there's a whole tradition

00:27:32.610 --> 00:27:35.230
both in modern scholarship
and in older scholarship

00:27:35.230 --> 00:27:38.610
of trying to read Jewish theology,

00:27:38.610 --> 00:27:40.690
the history of Jewish textual tradition

00:27:40.690 --> 00:27:42.390
through the language of survival.

00:27:42.390 --> 00:27:46.170
And so the stakes for me are
to disarticulate these things

00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:50.600
and suggest that the language
of survival actually emerges

00:27:51.450 --> 00:27:54.340
not only from a Christian
theological tradition

00:27:54.340 --> 00:27:59.340
but actually captures Jews
as a theological category

00:27:59.750 --> 00:28:02.180
through the language of survival,

00:28:02.180 --> 00:28:04.680
through the Augustinian
tradition and so forth.

00:28:04.680 --> 00:28:09.210
And it's this, call it a
theological political reorientation

00:28:09.210 --> 00:28:11.290
but also a geopolitical reorientation

00:28:11.290 --> 00:28:14.480
to the extent that terms
like Judeo-Christian

00:28:14.480 --> 00:28:16.430
I think gloss over

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:19.800
the stakes of thinking about

00:28:21.060 --> 00:28:22.990
what am I mean to set

00:28:25.630 --> 00:28:28.840
Jewishness or the history
of Jewish textual tradition,

00:28:28.840 --> 00:28:30.350
whatever it might be off

00:28:30.350 --> 00:28:34.070
from the kind of obviousness
of survival as a link

00:28:34.070 --> 00:28:34.903
between them.

00:28:35.950 --> 00:28:37.810
- No, I think it's a great discovery

00:28:37.810 --> 00:28:40.630
and it's really useful for understanding

00:28:40.630 --> 00:28:43.003
how one is read through the other.

00:28:44.080 --> 00:28:47.100
I guess I would say and I
wonder if you agree here

00:28:47.100 --> 00:28:49.880
that it's a particular interpretation

00:28:49.880 --> 00:28:54.603
of a relationship to loss,
a recuperative relationship

00:28:55.530 --> 00:28:57.820
and I can see how the
structures that you bring in

00:28:57.820 --> 00:29:01.740
to understand survival, the Eucharist,

00:29:01.740 --> 00:29:06.160
the passion of Christ, God as
the ultimate ever lived and

00:29:06.160 --> 00:29:08.900
survivor who has eternal life.

00:29:08.900 --> 00:29:10.340
And then you raise the wonderful question,

00:29:10.340 --> 00:29:11.640
well, what is life?

00:29:11.640 --> 00:29:14.910
It really depends what you mean by life

00:29:14.910 --> 00:29:16.700
when you're saying someone survives.

00:29:16.700 --> 00:29:17.593
I think of,

00:29:19.170 --> 00:29:20.350
Summer Morgan Stern

00:29:20.350 --> 00:29:22.690
who was a friend of Benjamin's
who came to New York

00:29:22.690 --> 00:29:27.190
after the war and he had survived
but he really didn't feel

00:29:27.190 --> 00:29:29.140
like that word was the right word.

00:29:29.140 --> 00:29:32.383
It felt like the most thin of existences.

00:29:35.510 --> 00:29:38.720
So, well, I wanna ask
what you think about that

00:29:38.720 --> 00:29:41.450
as a certain interpretation of loss.

00:29:41.450 --> 00:29:46.450
And again if you have an
alternative that you wanna propose.

00:29:50.110 --> 00:29:50.943
- Right.

00:29:50.943 --> 00:29:52.713
I mean, I think that's right.

00:29:53.570 --> 00:29:55.200
In all of the examples

00:29:56.320 --> 00:29:58.490
that I give it's structured

00:29:58.490 --> 00:30:02.050
in some way around this question of loss.

00:30:02.050 --> 00:30:07.050
And things like the Eucharist,
God's survival, so forth

00:30:07.060 --> 00:30:11.513
are reactions to this
possibility to finitude and loss.

00:30:16.670 --> 00:30:17.503
So,

00:30:18.720 --> 00:30:23.010
the question I think for
me is not whether survival

00:30:23.010 --> 00:30:27.153
is so much a good description
of this question of loss.

00:30:28.310 --> 00:30:31.283
As you say, it does a lot of work.

00:30:32.160 --> 00:30:35.080
But for me I think the question is how

00:30:36.360 --> 00:30:37.620
central

00:30:37.620 --> 00:30:40.940
this question of survival
becomes in relationship to loss

00:30:40.940 --> 00:30:43.750
for understanding subjectivity in general.

00:30:43.750 --> 00:30:46.840
And to me, that's not a clear leap

00:30:46.840 --> 00:30:49.160
and certainly the Freudian tradition,

00:30:49.160 --> 00:30:52.660
even up through contemporary theory,

00:30:52.660 --> 00:30:54.530
Judith Butler and so forth,

00:30:54.530 --> 00:30:57.780
this link say between melancholia,
triumphant melancholia

00:30:57.780 --> 00:31:00.270
and the constitution of the subject,

00:31:00.270 --> 00:31:02.770
I think is something that
should just at least be,

00:31:02.770 --> 00:31:05.660
thought about in this genealogical way.

00:31:05.660 --> 00:31:08.420
And so while I do propose

00:31:08.420 --> 00:31:10.593
a kind of alternative at some point,

00:31:13.040 --> 00:31:15.850
my preliminary goal with
the book is to raise

00:31:15.850 --> 00:31:18.680
these questions as perhaps
being more contingent than

00:31:20.480 --> 00:31:22.023
they would sometimes suggest.

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:25.850
And so the next step then
would be to think about

00:31:26.840 --> 00:31:28.470
what an alternative might look like.

00:31:28.470 --> 00:31:32.773
Though I will leave that to
scholars more ambitious than I.

00:31:33.610 --> 00:31:35.270
- Yeah.

00:31:35.270 --> 00:31:37.090
Maybe I'll just ask you one more question

00:31:37.090 --> 00:31:39.410
and that's about maybe the most peculiar

00:31:39.410 --> 00:31:42.080
and complicated figure
of survival in your book.

00:31:42.080 --> 00:31:44.100
And that is I think,

00:31:44.100 --> 00:31:46.840
Benjamin's idea of the
fortleben of a text,

00:31:46.840 --> 00:31:50.037
the living forth of a text
into it's (indistinct),

00:31:51.740 --> 00:31:55.393
it's post ripeness or the
ripeness of its post age.

00:31:56.670 --> 00:31:59.007
This is certainly not a triumphalist view

00:32:00.754 --> 00:32:01.780
and it is

00:32:02.800 --> 00:32:07.200
not really about human
beings in any of the senses

00:32:07.200 --> 00:32:11.680
as quasi theological beings,
as imitations of Christ.

00:32:11.680 --> 00:32:13.553
How do you see that fitting in?

00:32:16.770 --> 00:32:17.730
- Right.

00:32:17.730 --> 00:32:18.620
So I think you're right.

00:32:18.620 --> 00:32:20.710
I think the chapter on Benjamin

00:32:21.910 --> 00:32:24.420
stands in an interesting
relationship to the chapter

00:32:24.420 --> 00:32:25.550
that proceeds it, Arendt,

00:32:25.550 --> 00:32:29.290
which is about political
questions and so forth.

00:32:29.290 --> 00:32:32.220
And some of the theological work that I do

00:32:32.220 --> 00:32:34.800
in the rest of the book afterward,

00:32:34.800 --> 00:32:37.730
specifically in relationship
to things concerning

00:32:37.730 --> 00:32:40.480
Christ's body and the Eucharist so forth.

00:32:40.480 --> 00:32:42.640
For me, Benjamin did do a couple things.

00:32:42.640 --> 00:32:44.470
One is that to the extent

00:32:44.470 --> 00:32:46.970
that there is a contemporary
philosophical discourse

00:32:46.970 --> 00:32:51.050
about survival, it
emerges in a strange way

00:32:52.230 --> 00:32:56.280
in relationship to Benjamin's
text on translation.

00:32:56.280 --> 00:32:58.470
This apparent distinction,

00:32:58.470 --> 00:33:00.380
it's not even clear Benjamin
makes a distinction,

00:33:00.380 --> 00:33:02.910
but Derrida suggested at some point

00:33:02.910 --> 00:33:04.330
that there was a distinction

00:33:04.330 --> 00:33:07.416
between fortleben and uberleben

00:33:07.416 --> 00:33:10.803
and that one could map
these along different lines,

00:33:10.803 --> 00:33:13.053
living on versus living after.

00:33:14.770 --> 00:33:18.930
So for one thing, that
linguistic difference

00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:22.460
and its afterlife in
contemporary philosophy

00:33:22.460 --> 00:33:25.770
was helpful for orienting the
problem as one of translation.

00:33:25.770 --> 00:33:29.240
That one of the issues with
survival is that I wanted to use

00:33:29.240 --> 00:33:33.910
translation to make it less
transparent as a category,

00:33:33.910 --> 00:33:36.310
and to think about the
way that across languages

00:33:37.230 --> 00:33:39.840
translation creates problems
for thinking about exactly

00:33:39.840 --> 00:33:41.563
what survival was doing.

00:33:42.620 --> 00:33:45.960
But in doing that, what I try to show

00:33:45.960 --> 00:33:48.400
is that as much as Benjamin writes

00:33:48.400 --> 00:33:51.113
about the fortleben of the text,

00:33:52.150 --> 00:33:54.290
uberleben somehow

00:33:54.290 --> 00:33:58.040
has a less clear set of uses.

00:33:58.040 --> 00:33:59.870
And yet what I try to suggest

00:33:59.870 --> 00:34:02.460
is that the term uberleben
itself, or that Benjamin's text

00:34:02.460 --> 00:34:04.900
is an opportunity for
thinking about the history

00:34:04.900 --> 00:34:07.450
of the translation of that word

00:34:07.450 --> 00:34:09.430
and the way that Benjamin uses it.

00:34:09.430 --> 00:34:11.730
And that there are ways of
reading Benjamin's texts

00:34:11.730 --> 00:34:14.080
especially toward the end with the notion

00:34:14.080 --> 00:34:17.820
of a kind of performative language that

00:34:19.430 --> 00:34:22.060
is translatable less as survival

00:34:22.060 --> 00:34:23.360
or translatable as survival,

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:25.830
but in a particular sense
of kind of hyper life

00:34:25.830 --> 00:34:26.893
or super life.

00:34:28.030 --> 00:34:29.850
And what I tried to show as you mentioned

00:34:29.850 --> 00:34:34.617
is that my hypothesis is this (indistinct)

00:34:35.710 --> 00:34:37.180
back to

00:34:38.500 --> 00:34:40.630
the texts of Pseudo-Dionysius,

00:34:40.630 --> 00:34:43.250
where the idea of super life

00:34:43.250 --> 00:34:47.060
is a description of God's
sovereignties or apathetically,

00:34:47.060 --> 00:34:51.820
registered but that this
construction of uberleben

00:34:51.820 --> 00:34:53.980
as hyper life one can actually follow

00:34:53.980 --> 00:34:55.450
a history of translation

00:34:55.450 --> 00:34:57.880
through translations of Pseudo-Dionysius's

00:34:57.880 --> 00:34:59.210
texts into German.

00:34:59.210 --> 00:35:01.670
So Benjamin does

00:35:01.670 --> 00:35:06.670
some work for me linguistically
and archivally, I would say

00:35:07.720 --> 00:35:09.483
in preparation for the rest.

00:35:10.630 --> 00:35:12.320
- Right, I found it a
really interesting chapter.

00:35:12.320 --> 00:35:14.350
And you come back to
Benjamin later to talk

00:35:14.350 --> 00:35:17.450
about the aura as a form of survival.

00:35:17.450 --> 00:35:20.710
I guess I would say that
one valence of survival

00:35:20.710 --> 00:35:25.080
is this again, in a
kind of particular spot

00:35:25.080 --> 00:35:29.070
in a Christian tradition that
there's some precious stuff

00:35:29.070 --> 00:35:31.803
call it spirit that has carried over.

00:35:32.710 --> 00:35:34.320
But Benjamin when he comes to it,

00:35:34.320 --> 00:35:36.900
really thinks nothing is carried over.

00:35:36.900 --> 00:35:38.180
That's the interesting part of it,

00:35:38.180 --> 00:35:42.370
that the translation touches
the original at a certain part,

00:35:42.370 --> 00:35:45.053
but there isn't a preservation there.

00:35:46.870 --> 00:35:48.300
So, it could offer

00:35:49.710 --> 00:35:52.060
another model where instead of surviving

00:35:52.060 --> 00:35:54.453
you're in fact departing
from a certain spot.

00:35:58.231 --> 00:36:00.743
- Right, no, I think this is right and,

00:36:03.660 --> 00:36:06.420
I mean, obviously people like

00:36:07.740 --> 00:36:09.030
Sam Weber and so forth

00:36:09.030 --> 00:36:11.290
have kind of opened up this term fortleben

00:36:11.290 --> 00:36:14.160
in interesting ways and this departing

00:36:14.160 --> 00:36:17.000
from a text into the text
of another and so forth.

00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:21.293
And I think these readings
of Benjamin are all correct.

00:36:25.430 --> 00:36:27.720
What's interesting actually
is in that chapter,

00:36:27.720 --> 00:36:30.470
in the particular valence
that uberleben takes on

00:36:30.470 --> 00:36:31.440
is it's actually not clear

00:36:31.440 --> 00:36:33.563
that it's about preservation at all.

00:36:34.490 --> 00:36:37.970
And then in some sense, it's
a tempt to actually understand

00:36:37.970 --> 00:36:40.910
what do we mean to have a
language without content

00:36:40.910 --> 00:36:44.720
that preserves nothing
but it's actually creative

00:36:44.720 --> 00:36:46.340
as creative in some

00:36:48.780 --> 00:36:50.390
powerful way.

00:36:50.390 --> 00:36:53.050
And this is what I think
it does for I'm thinking

00:36:53.050 --> 00:36:57.310
about this term, uberleben
and it's difference

00:36:57.310 --> 00:37:01.560
if it is one from the way
that fortleben tracks this,

00:37:01.560 --> 00:37:05.003
becoming other texts across
the rest of the essay.

00:37:07.320 --> 00:37:08.240
- Well, terrific.

00:37:08.240 --> 00:37:10.160
Thank you for talking with me.

00:37:10.160 --> 00:37:12.610
I'm gonna open up the floor to questions.

00:37:12.610 --> 00:37:15.079
- Thank you Paul, I appreciate it.

00:37:15.079 --> 00:37:16.730
- It's a total pleasure.

00:37:16.730 --> 00:37:20.163
I'm going to turn to the
Q&amp;A now and I'm gonna,

00:37:22.180 --> 00:37:24.450
well, since I didn't ask whether
people wanted to be named

00:37:24.450 --> 00:37:25.900
I guess I won't name the people

00:37:25.900 --> 00:37:28.963
who are asking but I
will ask your questions.

00:37:30.010 --> 00:37:33.520
Here's a question about
Christianity as a question

00:37:33.520 --> 00:37:36.513
and which is the Christianity
you're talking about?

00:37:39.500 --> 00:37:42.260
He wants to know, or she wants to know

00:37:44.490 --> 00:37:47.443
what is the state of the
Christian question in your work?

00:37:50.270 --> 00:37:54.120
- Yes, it remains a question
or at least I think if

00:37:56.560 --> 00:37:59.550
my understanding of the state
of the Christian question

00:37:59.550 --> 00:38:02.000
is correct is that it is a question

00:38:02.000 --> 00:38:04.110
that maybe is

00:38:06.040 --> 00:38:08.950
still burgeoning strangely enough,

00:38:08.950 --> 00:38:12.410
that it still needs to be
asked in a particular way.

00:38:12.410 --> 00:38:16.130
And my hope then is that
this question of survival

00:38:16.130 --> 00:38:19.600
is a way of opening up what
that question might be,

00:38:19.600 --> 00:38:23.453
theological, political,
economic representational.

00:38:24.640 --> 00:38:26.500
Paul and I didn't have a
chance to talk about media

00:38:26.500 --> 00:38:29.040
but one of the frames for
thinking about the survivor

00:38:29.040 --> 00:38:30.780
as a figure is thinking about survival

00:38:30.780 --> 00:38:34.770
actually in relationship to
things like absence and presence

00:38:34.770 --> 00:38:37.790
as they appear in both contemporary media

00:38:37.790 --> 00:38:40.050
and then historically in debates

00:38:40.050 --> 00:38:42.423
about representation in Christianity.

00:38:46.237 --> 00:38:49.012
So, yes, so survival is a way I would say

00:38:49.012 --> 00:38:52.723
opposing this question and
keeping it open as a question.

00:38:55.780 --> 00:38:59.060
- Okay, I'm gonna go
onto another question.

00:38:59.060 --> 00:39:01.070
This one says, in the opening of your book

00:39:01.070 --> 00:39:03.303
you identify the Jewish survival,

00:39:03.303 --> 00:39:07.220
a genealogy of survival
(indistinct) can look differently

00:39:07.220 --> 00:39:09.330
and less Christian if
originated in the Jew

00:39:09.330 --> 00:39:10.597
as a "stranger".

00:39:11.980 --> 00:39:13.560
In modernity the lineage arise

00:39:13.560 --> 00:39:16.990
from Zimmel via Boober
Benjamin and Arendt et cetera.

00:39:16.990 --> 00:39:18.990
The stranger may point
towards a different ethics

00:39:18.990 --> 00:39:20.742
in theo-politics of survival

00:39:20.742 --> 00:39:23.373
and the Christianized reading by Agamben.

00:39:25.070 --> 00:39:26.753
Curious to hear your thoughts.

00:39:28.280 --> 00:39:31.040
- So the question is, is
whether the stranger is

00:39:32.610 --> 00:39:37.260
potentially more productive
figure than survival?

00:39:37.260 --> 00:39:40.150
- It seems that there
is a survival attached

00:39:40.150 --> 00:39:42.000
to this stranger

00:39:42.000 --> 00:39:44.953
and it might have a different
ethics associated with it.

00:39:49.150 --> 00:39:50.411
- It might, I don't know.

00:39:50.411 --> 00:39:53.083
I haven't thought too much
about the stranger, but,

00:39:55.550 --> 00:39:58.153
it's an interesting pairing with survival.

00:40:00.270 --> 00:40:02.440
I'll have to think a
little bit more about that.

00:40:02.440 --> 00:40:06.360
I don't have anything
concrete to say at the moment.

00:40:06.360 --> 00:40:08.790
- In your portrayals the survivor,

00:40:08.790 --> 00:40:12.140
except maybe for Benjamin
is always our survivor.

00:40:12.140 --> 00:40:14.220
The survivor of our group,

00:40:14.220 --> 00:40:17.500
the survivor that belongs somewhere.

00:40:17.500 --> 00:40:19.100
And that carries the belonging with them

00:40:19.100 --> 00:40:23.023
even into an alien
territory exiles for one.

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:25.633
- Right, I mean,

00:40:26.890 --> 00:40:29.793
this reminds me, this
is helpful, actually.

00:40:32.340 --> 00:40:33.893
One question that,

00:40:35.790 --> 00:40:37.140
the question of political theology

00:40:37.140 --> 00:40:40.760
of survival brings up in relationship

00:40:40.760 --> 00:40:44.690
to kind of famous things in
the field of (indistinct)

00:40:44.690 --> 00:40:46.420
and so forth is the
degree to which survival

00:40:46.420 --> 00:40:50.810
is actually is always a
function of a community,

00:40:50.810 --> 00:40:54.810
the self and the other
multiplicity and individuality.

00:40:54.810 --> 00:40:57.330
And so thinking about
what the stranger might be

00:40:57.330 --> 00:40:58.930
in relationship to the way that survival

00:40:58.930 --> 00:41:01.700
always forms a community of
saying inclusion and exclusion

00:41:01.700 --> 00:41:02.610
could be an interesting way

00:41:02.610 --> 00:41:05.020
of thinking about opening that up

00:41:05.020 --> 00:41:07.520
but I'd have to give it a
little bit more thought.

00:41:08.700 --> 00:41:12.720
- Yeah, you write in chapter
four on the sovereign

00:41:12.720 --> 00:41:15.250
about figures of sovereignty

00:41:15.250 --> 00:41:17.350
even in the age of pure sovereignty,

00:41:17.350 --> 00:41:21.310
absolute sovereignty as being
kind of survivors of God.

00:41:21.310 --> 00:41:24.670
And in a sense, the sovereign
has to survive into modernity,

00:41:24.670 --> 00:41:27.500
sovereignty does in other forms.

00:41:27.500 --> 00:41:30.550
The one Christ who says this is my body,

00:41:30.550 --> 00:41:32.260
the sovereign who says

00:41:32.260 --> 00:41:34.540
(speaking in foreign language)

00:41:34.540 --> 00:41:37.400
has to take different forms in modernity

00:41:37.400 --> 00:41:42.400
and already that you show
is active in "Hamlet"

00:41:42.470 --> 00:41:44.777
where you do a marvelous
reading of "Hamlet".

00:41:47.840 --> 00:41:51.083
I wonder if you think those forums are,

00:41:53.180 --> 00:41:55.820
proliferating if you see them in places

00:41:55.820 --> 00:41:57.343
as you look around now.

00:41:58.470 --> 00:42:00.950
The ones that call back
to a theological model

00:42:00.950 --> 00:42:05.083
that are the gods that you
believe in, in disguise.

00:42:08.010 --> 00:42:12.710
- Well, I'm gonna keep this on
a bit of a theoretical level,

00:42:12.710 --> 00:42:15.720
but I have been wondering recently.

00:42:15.720 --> 00:42:18.680
And I'm actually not sure
I'm committed to this claim,

00:42:18.680 --> 00:42:20.610
so I'll be curious to
hear what you think, Paul,

00:42:20.610 --> 00:42:24.200
but one way of talking
about the one popular way

00:42:24.200 --> 00:42:26.110
coming out of Foucault for
talking about the difference

00:42:26.110 --> 00:42:28.270
between epox of say power

00:42:28.270 --> 00:42:32.370
would be sovereignty as
a kind of singular forum.

00:42:32.370 --> 00:42:33.683
And then something like,

00:42:36.450 --> 00:42:37.370
you have to make die

00:42:37.370 --> 00:42:40.600
and live versus to make live and let die.

00:42:40.600 --> 00:42:45.600
But if I'm in the classical
absolute sovereign sense,

00:42:45.820 --> 00:42:49.160
the sovereign is already a
kind of Eucharistic body,

00:42:49.160 --> 00:42:51.230
it's already making live.

00:42:51.230 --> 00:42:55.410
The community is already
sustained in some way

00:42:57.605 --> 00:42:58.640
by the king's body.

00:42:58.640 --> 00:43:00.550
So it is possible that

00:43:03.167 --> 00:43:05.810
the very periodization that Foucault

00:43:05.810 --> 00:43:08.450
uses is actually bound up some way with

00:43:10.050 --> 00:43:13.370
this strange history of
the king's three bodies

00:43:13.370 --> 00:43:17.360
or four bodies or however
many bodies it may have.

00:43:17.360 --> 00:43:19.700
- Yeah, I guess in Europe
the French revolution

00:43:19.700 --> 00:43:21.800
would be a good turning point there.

00:43:21.800 --> 00:43:24.700
If the sovereign can carry

00:43:24.700 --> 00:43:27.590
the survival problem
of God for the people,

00:43:27.590 --> 00:43:28.690
the people are really free

00:43:28.690 --> 00:43:30.490
but once the people have to carry it

00:43:31.610 --> 00:43:34.650
and have to continually
support the sovereign

00:43:34.650 --> 00:43:37.963
through their will then you
have to make the people survive.

00:43:40.130 --> 00:43:42.530
It's a thought, I'm going
back to the questions.

00:43:45.530 --> 00:43:48.130
Yes, the question is,
could you talk a little bit

00:43:48.130 --> 00:43:50.120
or does your book talk
about the etymologies

00:43:50.120 --> 00:43:52.610
and different linguistic
histories of the Latinate versions

00:43:52.610 --> 00:43:55.742
of survival and the
dramatic terms that Arendt,

00:43:55.742 --> 00:43:58.120
Benjamin, Rosenzweig use,
uberleben, fortleben.

00:43:58.120 --> 00:43:59.560
What are the important differences

00:43:59.560 --> 00:44:01.060
and could you talk about that?

00:44:02.100 --> 00:44:03.873
- Right, so it does.

00:44:05.590 --> 00:44:07.140
So the differences between

00:44:08.890 --> 00:44:09.723
say,

00:44:09.723 --> 00:44:12.850
German, French and English
appear throughout to the extent

00:44:12.850 --> 00:44:16.263
that I'm reading texts that
move through these languages.

00:44:17.780 --> 00:44:21.580
The Latin distinctions
are interesting as well.

00:44:21.580 --> 00:44:26.150
I actually, I developed this
a little bit less in the book

00:44:26.150 --> 00:44:27.940
than elsewhere, but

00:44:30.150 --> 00:44:34.923
etymologically people like
Benjamin and so forth,

00:44:36.290 --> 00:44:37.670
the term survival

00:44:37.670 --> 00:44:42.380
has often been linked to
superstition or superstare.

00:44:42.380 --> 00:44:44.470
And so there's actually a kind of dual

00:44:44.470 --> 00:44:47.700
and sort of unclear etymology
of where the English term

00:44:47.700 --> 00:44:50.440
or the French term survival comes from.

00:44:50.440 --> 00:44:51.273
And,

00:44:52.310 --> 00:44:55.000
it hasn't really been looked at

00:44:55.000 --> 00:44:58.070
I think from historical
linguistic standpoint

00:44:58.070 --> 00:45:00.490
but much like religion,

00:45:00.490 --> 00:45:04.400
there's a kind of a doubling
of survival's genealogy

00:45:04.400 --> 00:45:08.777
that remains unclear as to
the sort of (indistinct).

00:45:10.640 --> 00:45:13.950
I bring this out somewhat in the book but

00:45:15.470 --> 00:45:17.733
focus more on one than the other.

00:45:19.263 --> 00:45:21.510
- Here's another question towards Benjamin

00:45:22.840 --> 00:45:25.540
about super life, hyper
life and translation.

00:45:25.540 --> 00:45:28.870
Is the super life of a
text, not it's survival

00:45:28.870 --> 00:45:30.390
but it's fracture.

00:45:30.390 --> 00:45:32.340
Is fracture a different mode of survival

00:45:32.340 --> 00:45:35.453
that departs from the Christian
theological in position?

00:45:41.100 --> 00:45:41.980
- That sounds possible.

00:45:41.980 --> 00:45:45.050
Yes, I mean, I would
need to think about that

00:45:45.050 --> 00:45:48.243
a little bit more in
relationship to Benjamin's text.

00:45:49.330 --> 00:45:50.163
I mean,

00:45:53.280 --> 00:45:55.543
the claim about the term uberleben

00:45:58.240 --> 00:46:01.340
in Benjamin's text of the translator essay

00:46:01.340 --> 00:46:04.420
is that actually he's
borrowing the way that he uses

00:46:04.420 --> 00:46:06.510
the term in his essay on romanticism

00:46:06.510 --> 00:46:10.850
as a kind of higher life but
that this isn't necessarily

00:46:10.850 --> 00:46:12.380
the higher life of the text.

00:46:12.380 --> 00:46:13.370
It's the

00:46:15.640 --> 00:46:18.100
moment at the very end of the essay

00:46:18.100 --> 00:46:21.710
when Benjamin tries to articulate the

00:46:22.970 --> 00:46:25.390
holy text or holy,

00:46:25.390 --> 00:46:28.870
the Bible as a text that
doesn't need to be translated

00:46:28.870 --> 00:46:32.410
because it's already

00:46:32.410 --> 00:46:33.593
freely translated

00:46:33.593 --> 00:46:36.100
in the sense that it
actually produces language

00:46:36.100 --> 00:46:39.880
as opposed to its derivative in some way.

00:46:39.880 --> 00:46:41.343
And so uberleben

00:46:42.640 --> 00:46:43.473
stands there.

00:46:43.473 --> 00:46:44.510
And so this question of fracture

00:46:44.510 --> 00:46:47.640
across the rest of the essay
it would be interesting to read

00:46:47.640 --> 00:46:51.020
those two moments more
intention than I do in the text

00:46:51.020 --> 00:46:53.400
and think about whether
that question of fracture

00:46:53.400 --> 00:46:55.180
could depart in some way from

00:46:56.560 --> 00:46:58.150
the

00:46:58.150 --> 00:47:00.460
link between sovereignty and uberleben,

00:47:00.460 --> 00:47:01.573
their wholeness.

00:47:04.200 --> 00:47:05.583
- Okay, here's a question.

00:47:05.583 --> 00:47:06.416
Thank you.

00:47:07.990 --> 00:47:09.630
This is a question about language.

00:47:09.630 --> 00:47:12.520
Another one, there's a
lot of interest in this.

00:47:12.520 --> 00:47:14.250
If you premise the book upon the question

00:47:14.250 --> 00:47:16.293
who is speaking about survival,

00:47:17.560 --> 00:47:21.353
this here is wondering
about the preposition about,

00:47:22.280 --> 00:47:24.190
can you separate language and speaking

00:47:24.190 --> 00:47:27.400
as a medium of articulation
from survival as its object?

00:47:27.400 --> 00:47:31.120
Wouldn't the speaking and the
language be a form of survival

00:47:31.120 --> 00:47:34.930
following Benjamin, Derrida, Hammacher.

00:47:34.930 --> 00:47:37.750
Can you talk about survival before talking

00:47:37.750 --> 00:47:40.023
about the survival structure of talking?

00:47:41.930 --> 00:47:42.763
- Right.

00:47:42.763 --> 00:47:45.460
I think that sounds right to me.

00:47:45.460 --> 00:47:48.780
Though if I'm understanding
the question correctly,

00:47:48.780 --> 00:47:53.110
there seems to be kind
of say, Freud opposition

00:47:53.110 --> 00:47:57.090
between what does it mean
to think about survival as a

00:47:57.090 --> 00:47:59.253
particular word, term,

00:48:01.700 --> 00:48:03.140
part of a particular language,

00:48:03.140 --> 00:48:04.770
a particular translation history,

00:48:04.770 --> 00:48:07.800
and what do I mean to generalize survival

00:48:07.800 --> 00:48:10.600
as a kind of universalizing structure

00:48:10.600 --> 00:48:13.100
for what it means to
speak in the first place?

00:48:13.100 --> 00:48:17.370
And obviously in the book I
take the kind of first take,

00:48:17.370 --> 00:48:19.440
I think that Derrida does

00:48:22.360 --> 00:48:25.350
sort of 30 year project
of generalizing survival

00:48:25.350 --> 00:48:27.830
has its own strategies behind it.

00:48:27.830 --> 00:48:29.510
That survival can't be understood

00:48:29.510 --> 00:48:31.360
necessarily the kind of
exceptional position,

00:48:31.360 --> 00:48:36.020
but needs to be understood
as a kind of substructure

00:48:36.020 --> 00:48:39.303
for thinking about
language, life, in general,

00:48:42.142 --> 00:48:43.757
which is very interesting, I think

00:48:43.757 --> 00:48:45.770
and in some ways the inspiration

00:48:45.770 --> 00:48:48.170
for the way that this book works.

00:48:48.170 --> 00:48:51.110
But I try to go in a different
direction by thinking

00:48:51.110 --> 00:48:54.120
about how even that
generalization operates

00:48:54.120 --> 00:48:57.310
perhaps within a particular
closure or particular history

00:48:57.310 --> 00:49:01.032
of say metaphysics, Christian
theology, Christianity.

00:49:01.032 --> 00:49:04.090
And so in that sense, it
would try to provisionalize

00:49:04.090 --> 00:49:06.550
this claim about the
generalization of survival

00:49:07.800 --> 00:49:09.930
as a structure of speaking.

00:49:09.930 --> 00:49:12.030
Again, if I'm understanding this question.

00:49:13.220 --> 00:49:14.053
- Yeah, I agree.

00:49:14.053 --> 00:49:16.360
It would seem to me that it's begging

00:49:16.360 --> 00:49:21.360
the question to impose survival
and language, but it's true.

00:49:21.440 --> 00:49:22.500
The questioner is right,

00:49:22.500 --> 00:49:26.253
that there is a kind of
circular issue there, and maybe,

00:49:27.930 --> 00:49:32.050
it really depends on whether
we can speak differently

00:49:32.050 --> 00:49:33.230
out of our past.

00:49:33.230 --> 00:49:36.780
That is to use language
differently before we can start

00:49:36.780 --> 00:49:38.823
to talk about survival differently.

00:49:40.130 --> 00:49:41.410
I wanna ask you now,

00:49:41.410 --> 00:49:43.647
I think we've come to the
end of our questions for now

00:49:43.647 --> 00:49:46.680
but people are welcome to ask a few more.

00:49:46.680 --> 00:49:48.123
We have a few minutes left.

00:49:49.000 --> 00:49:51.370
I wanna ask you about psychology

00:49:52.930 --> 00:49:55.020
because there's a lot of, first of all,

00:49:55.020 --> 00:49:59.000
psychological attachment
to the idea of survival

00:49:59.000 --> 00:50:01.830
but psychology is imagined in Freud

00:50:01.830 --> 00:50:05.573
as you deal with him as a kind
of survival from childhood.

00:50:07.020 --> 00:50:10.223
Can you talk about trauma
as one model of this?

00:50:11.130 --> 00:50:13.030
Maybe you could talk a little bit about

00:50:13.030 --> 00:50:17.200
the difference between
the psychiatric invention

00:50:17.200 --> 00:50:20.960
of the concept of the
survivor by Robert Jay Lifton

00:50:20.960 --> 00:50:25.780
which you address in your
prologue, your introduction

00:50:25.780 --> 00:50:30.180
and Freud's trying to come to
terms with rests and retinols

00:50:30.180 --> 00:50:33.563
and remainders, that seemed
to structure the psyche.

00:50:36.760 --> 00:50:38.670
- Right, so I'm gonna try
to answer this question

00:50:38.670 --> 00:50:41.940
a little bit obliquely, I think,

00:50:41.940 --> 00:50:44.860
which is that one of the
arguments of the book

00:50:45.870 --> 00:50:49.510
is that already in Freud's lifetime

00:50:49.510 --> 00:50:52.390
but really in the post-war
period, people like Lifton

00:50:52.390 --> 00:50:54.170
but then also important trauma theorists

00:50:54.170 --> 00:50:59.170
like Cathy Caruth and so
forth use survival to describe

00:50:59.290 --> 00:51:04.290
a whole series of things that
emerge out of psychoanalysis.

00:51:04.910 --> 00:51:05.743
And,

00:51:07.760 --> 00:51:10.450
the question I wanna
ask is whether survival

00:51:10.450 --> 00:51:13.740
actually, as a term,
is a Freudian concept.

00:51:13.740 --> 00:51:16.420
Which is to say in
Freud's texts is survival,

00:51:16.420 --> 00:51:18.800
a concept like say the reality principle

00:51:18.800 --> 00:51:21.950
or the death drive something
that Freud reflects on

00:51:21.950 --> 00:51:26.170
as a particular category in
need of further examination.

00:51:26.170 --> 00:51:29.460
And the turn then to this,

00:51:29.460 --> 00:51:32.430
I read a short dream

00:51:32.430 --> 00:51:35.993
that Freud looks at in the
interpretation of dreams,

00:51:37.060 --> 00:51:42.060
is a place where he actually
is invested in the language of

00:51:43.647 --> 00:51:48.100
uberleben or actually
works with a particular

00:51:48.100 --> 00:51:51.590
word as opposed to it being
imposed on the Freudian corpus.

00:51:51.590 --> 00:51:54.480
And what emerges from that
is this kind of strange,

00:51:54.480 --> 00:51:57.690
transferential dialectic
between a kind of search

00:51:57.690 --> 00:52:01.400
for a sovereign triumphalism,
what it means to stand

00:52:03.910 --> 00:52:07.130
at a grave over the lost of a loved one.

00:52:07.130 --> 00:52:09.193
And the sense that actually,

00:52:10.350 --> 00:52:12.820
there are no sovereign
singular individuals.

00:52:12.820 --> 00:52:17.010
We are all kind of ghostly replications of

00:52:18.460 --> 00:52:21.803
everyone else and that this
is Freud's great anxiety.

00:52:23.490 --> 00:52:26.080
And so the question for
me then is with Freud

00:52:26.080 --> 00:52:31.080
is to try to think linguistically
or in terms of translation

00:52:31.330 --> 00:52:35.990
about how important survival
has become to thinking

00:52:35.990 --> 00:52:38.203
about psychoanalysis
in the post-war period.

00:52:39.530 --> 00:52:42.410
And again, in figures like Caruth,

00:52:42.410 --> 00:52:46.000
how it's often linked to Jewish survival.

00:52:46.000 --> 00:52:49.010
That much of the potent force

00:52:49.010 --> 00:52:51.130
of that comes out of a reading
of Moses and monotheism.

00:52:51.130 --> 00:52:54.643
And so I try to move this back and suggest

00:52:54.643 --> 00:52:58.153
that Freud's actually
working with a series,

00:52:58.153 --> 00:52:59.510
a kind of Christian archive

00:52:59.510 --> 00:53:01.460
in that particular moment in the dream.

00:53:02.670 --> 00:53:05.150
- Yeah, the dream is really spectacular.

00:53:05.150 --> 00:53:06.550
Maybe I'll just say a little about it.

00:53:06.550 --> 00:53:09.590
We can talk about the dream
to close out the session.

00:53:09.590 --> 00:53:14.270
Freud has a dream in which
he is in the laboratory

00:53:14.270 --> 00:53:15.440
of his former teacher.

00:53:15.440 --> 00:53:16.630
He hears a knock on the door

00:53:16.630 --> 00:53:19.683
and the dead professor
Fleischer isn't there.

00:53:21.260 --> 00:53:24.320
Incidentally, it's a fleshy visitor

00:53:24.320 --> 00:53:26.663
whose name is Flesh,
basically middle flesh.

00:53:27.800 --> 00:53:30.700
And Freud goes through
meeting a whole set of people

00:53:30.700 --> 00:53:33.980
who have caused him trouble,
including priests who,

00:53:33.980 --> 00:53:38.960
it says, kind of ego,
ideal and (indistinct)

00:53:40.330 --> 00:53:43.670
and he's gripped by strange effects.

00:53:43.670 --> 00:53:47.120
He sees a sign that says non Vixen

00:53:48.090 --> 00:53:49.660
and,

00:53:49.660 --> 00:53:50.760
he ends up thinking

00:53:50.760 --> 00:53:53.697
that flesh had been only an
apparition to (indistinct).

00:53:55.197 --> 00:53:57.360
It's a very complicated
dream which you carry

00:53:57.360 --> 00:53:58.960
through the rest of the chapter.

00:54:00.510 --> 00:54:04.930
It seems to me that whatever
there is about survival here

00:54:04.930 --> 00:54:07.750
is really about rivalry.

00:54:07.750 --> 00:54:09.710
In a sense you were saying that.

00:54:09.710 --> 00:54:12.680
And I wonder if there's a real
cynical reading of survivor

00:54:12.680 --> 00:54:14.873
here which is something like,

00:54:16.220 --> 00:54:18.290
it is an imagined

00:54:20.300 --> 00:54:22.650
escape from struggle or an imagined

00:54:25.340 --> 00:54:27.730
triumph I guess in the
way we've been talking

00:54:27.730 --> 00:54:29.880
about it but now in a social sense

00:54:29.880 --> 00:54:32.440
where you could come out on
top without actually having to,

00:54:32.440 --> 00:54:35.363
you know you just kill
everybody in the death wish.

00:54:37.180 --> 00:54:38.013
- Right, right.

00:54:38.013 --> 00:54:40.590
No, I think it's this kind of dream,

00:54:40.590 --> 00:54:41.840
maybe it's a terrifying dream

00:54:41.840 --> 00:54:45.050
but dream of absolute
sovereignty somehow for Freud

00:54:45.050 --> 00:54:45.883
in this

00:54:47.080 --> 00:54:50.530
rivalrous mode, as you've been saying.

00:54:50.530 --> 00:54:53.460
And I think, you know, in terms
of the reception of Freud,

00:54:53.460 --> 00:54:56.230
this is more or less confetti's

00:54:56.230 --> 00:54:58.630
strange post-war
assessment of the survivor.

00:54:58.630 --> 00:55:00.860
Not that the survivor is a victim

00:55:00.860 --> 00:55:05.770
that needs to be thought of,
not in the sense of a victim

00:55:05.770 --> 00:55:08.790
but actually "mankind's worst evil"

00:55:08.790 --> 00:55:13.790
as he puts it in that book as
which he's taking from Freud,

00:55:13.820 --> 00:55:18.680
the survivor as this
triumphant, sovereign figure

00:55:18.680 --> 00:55:23.120
dreaming of killing all of those around.

00:55:23.120 --> 00:55:27.820
And this has a kind of reception
as well into people like

00:55:27.820 --> 00:55:30.270
Agamben and Cavalero and so forth.

00:55:30.270 --> 00:55:31.323
So this,

00:55:33.110 --> 00:55:36.200
peculiar language of sovereign survival

00:55:36.200 --> 00:55:38.940
is on, in Freud, has its
own kind of resonance

00:55:38.940 --> 00:55:40.870
into the present, I think.

00:55:40.870 --> 00:55:43.010
And his dream, I think is one place

00:55:43.010 --> 00:55:45.480
where you really see that happen.

00:55:45.480 --> 00:55:46.810
- There's as usual in Freud,

00:55:46.810 --> 00:55:49.360
there's a beautiful attack of conscience

00:55:49.360 --> 00:55:51.180
in that the repressed

00:55:52.140 --> 00:55:53.510
survives

00:55:53.510 --> 00:55:56.140
but it always comes
back as something else.

00:55:56.140 --> 00:55:59.580
So there's a disturbing
element to survival

00:55:59.580 --> 00:56:03.775
that doesn't allow you to
triumph over anything ever.

00:56:03.775 --> 00:56:05.890
- Right, right.

00:56:05.890 --> 00:56:07.940
- I don't know if you would
call this survival exactly,

00:56:07.940 --> 00:56:10.890
if something survives as
something that it never was.

00:56:10.890 --> 00:56:12.040
And at a certain point,

00:56:14.380 --> 00:56:16.030
I think it's Freud who talks about this

00:56:16.030 --> 00:56:21.030
either as a childhood memory
or the stuff of psychoanalysis

00:56:21.070 --> 00:56:22.363
or as a fantasy.

00:56:23.980 --> 00:56:27.450
So I wonder if you would
just help us end by talking

00:56:27.450 --> 00:56:32.450
about the kind of active and
phantasmagoric attachments

00:56:33.080 --> 00:56:36.100
to the idea of survival.

00:56:36.100 --> 00:56:37.290
It seems to me that

00:56:38.830 --> 00:56:40.520
it has a lot

00:56:40.520 --> 00:56:43.720
and it does a lot of work
psychologically even if not

00:56:45.370 --> 00:56:48.540
in the way that Lifton imagined.

00:56:48.540 --> 00:56:50.780
I don't know if you consider that at all

00:56:50.780 --> 00:56:53.440
or think about testimonies

00:56:54.820 --> 00:56:57.050
but I wonder if you could talk about that.

00:56:57.968 --> 00:57:00.400
- Can you say a little more Paul,

00:57:00.400 --> 00:57:03.920
sounds like you have a thought about this.

00:57:03.920 --> 00:57:05.423
- Well, I mean, in a way,

00:57:10.670 --> 00:57:11.800
I'm thinking that

00:57:14.780 --> 00:57:17.450
you do this great job of genealogizing

00:57:17.450 --> 00:57:19.220
and showing, here's a good example

00:57:20.230 --> 00:57:24.150
and showing how these German-Jewish,

00:57:24.150 --> 00:57:27.440
marginally Jewish,
marginally German figures

00:57:27.440 --> 00:57:32.440
who have vexed relationships
to both take up this term

00:57:32.620 --> 00:57:34.460
and how this offers a really good backdrop

00:57:34.460 --> 00:57:38.183
for maybe changing our
attachment to survivor for the,

00:57:39.970 --> 00:57:43.000
show, a discourse.

00:57:43.000 --> 00:57:45.010
But it does seem also that there are,

00:57:45.010 --> 00:57:47.240
let's say positive
dialectical moments to it

00:57:47.240 --> 00:57:49.270
and it allows you to process something

00:57:49.270 --> 00:57:54.010
or put something in a particular
place that as you say,

00:57:54.010 --> 00:57:56.370
it holds on to complexity

00:58:00.370 --> 00:58:03.740
as Freud as gripped by strange affects.

00:58:03.740 --> 00:58:07.800
In a sense, because nobody
knows what it means really,

00:58:07.800 --> 00:58:09.667
it's an ambiguous, highly ambiguous term

00:58:09.667 --> 00:58:13.143
and it might be doing the work
of maintaining the ambiguity,

00:58:15.990 --> 00:58:18.080
like the repressed does.

00:58:18.080 --> 00:58:19.813
The term keeps coming back.

00:58:22.100 --> 00:58:25.180
I wonder if you have a sense
of what its positive work is

00:58:25.180 --> 00:58:26.210
for let's say

00:58:27.590 --> 00:58:28.423
post show

00:58:29.750 --> 00:58:30.623
understanding.

00:58:31.530 --> 00:58:32.820
- Right, I mean, I think,

00:58:32.820 --> 00:58:36.540
even historically to the extent
that some significant work

00:58:36.540 --> 00:58:38.790
has been done on thinking about this

00:58:38.790 --> 00:58:41.433
in the post-Holocaust period,

00:58:43.460 --> 00:58:44.623
it's related to agency.

00:58:45.790 --> 00:58:48.540
This idea perhaps in certain contexts,

00:58:48.540 --> 00:58:49.730
in the post-war period,

00:58:49.730 --> 00:58:52.183
you have a kind of lack of resistance,

00:58:53.987 --> 00:58:57.370
"chiefs to the slaughter"
kind of accusations

00:58:57.370 --> 00:59:00.850
or perhaps collaboration
but negatively tinged.

00:59:00.850 --> 00:59:04.240
And survival provided a kind
of urgency for thinking about

00:59:04.240 --> 00:59:07.570
what it might mean to find strength

00:59:07.570 --> 00:59:09.923
in moments of suffering and so forth.

00:59:10.830 --> 00:59:14.200
So, yeah, I mean, survival has this...

00:59:15.810 --> 00:59:17.420
And as you said, because
it's so ambiguous,

00:59:17.420 --> 00:59:22.420
it holds together so much
that it has this flexibility

00:59:22.780 --> 00:59:26.480
to be used in all sorts
of different contexts,

00:59:26.480 --> 00:59:29.590
different ways not just
extreme historical situations

00:59:29.590 --> 00:59:31.763
but even in everyday life.

00:59:33.040 --> 00:59:34.290
I just like (indistinct),

00:59:37.780 --> 00:59:40.570
while it disperse on survivors,

00:59:40.570 --> 00:59:44.390
survival is tied to a particular context.

00:59:44.390 --> 00:59:47.090
It kind of colonizes
the whole field, right?

00:59:47.090 --> 00:59:50.910
If it's so ambiguous and it
takes up so much perpetrator

00:59:50.910 --> 00:59:54.430
and victim, ghostly,
multiplicity, and singularity,

00:59:54.430 --> 00:59:57.980
it ends up being an organizing
frame for thinking about,

00:59:57.980 --> 01:00:01.740
as I said earlier, subjectivity
in general in some way.

01:00:01.740 --> 01:00:05.380
And the question is whether
it really has exhausted

01:00:05.380 --> 01:00:09.510
in some way, all of the
dialectical possibilities

01:00:09.510 --> 01:00:13.180
or if there's something
effectively familiar

01:00:13.180 --> 01:00:16.560
about the term survival and
the figure of the survivor

01:00:16.560 --> 01:00:20.240
and that doing this
genealogical work could provide

01:00:20.240 --> 01:00:21.073
a kind of

01:00:22.920 --> 01:00:25.760
set of boundaries for thinking about

01:00:25.760 --> 01:00:27.930
what am I need to speak in different terms

01:00:27.930 --> 01:00:31.670
even if it's in say
non-Latinate languages.

01:00:31.670 --> 01:00:32.503
Other,

01:00:36.210 --> 01:00:38.310
discourses that have their own politics,

01:00:38.310 --> 01:00:40.920
their own say, materialism about them

01:00:40.920 --> 01:00:45.820
that don't produce the same
kind of images as survival does

01:00:45.820 --> 01:00:47.200
in their familiarity, at least.

01:00:47.200 --> 01:00:48.033
So,

01:00:51.021 --> 01:00:53.163
that's my sense of the issue.

